# 6.1810 Lock Lab — xv6

This repository is a fork of [MIT 6.1810 xv6-labs-2025](https://github.com/mit-pdos/xv6-labs-2025), branch `lock`, containing a solution to the [Lab: locks](https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/6.1810/2026/labs/lock.html) assignment.

## Disclosure

This solution was generated entirely by an AI model (DeepSeek V4 Pro / DeepSeek Max, driving [opencode](https://opencode.ai)). It was created as an intelligence test of the model, not as work completed by the repository owner. It is published for demonstration purposes; please do not copy it if you are taking the course.

## What was implemented

- `kernel/kalloc.c`: per-CPU free lists (one spinlock per CPU, all named "kmem"), with pages spread across CPUs at boot and O(1) whole-list stealing when a CPU's list is empty.
- `kernel/spinlock.h` / `kernel/spinlock.c`: an atomic-based reader-writer spinlock with writer priority and a wait-free reader path (no spinlock is taken by `read_acquire`).

`make grade` passes with a score of 100/100.

## Original README

---

xv6 is a re-implementation of Dennis Ritchie's and Ken Thompson's Unix
Version 6 (v6).  xv6 loosely follows the structure and style of v6,
but is implemented for a modern RISC-V multiprocessor using ANSI C.

ACKNOWLEDGMENTS

xv6 is inspired by John Lions's Commentary on UNIX 6th Edition (Peer
to Peer Communications; ISBN: 1-57398-013-7; 1st edition (June 14,
2000)).  See also https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/6.1810/, which provides
pointers to on-line resources for v6.

The following people have made contributions: Russ Cox (context switching,
locking), Cliff Frey (MP), Xiao Yu (MP), Nickolai Zeldovich, and Austin
Clements.

